<node id="Bosnia and Herzegovina">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Bosnia and Herzegovina is a country where Sarajevo is located and Istiklal Mosque was gifted to.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Istiqlal Mosque, or Masjid Istiqlal, (Independence Mosque) in Otoka, Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina is one of the largest mosques in the city.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Istiklal Mosque, Jakarta">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Istiklal Mosque, Jakarta is the national mosque of Indonesia and the namesake of the Istiklal Mosque in Sarajevo.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was named after Istiqlal Mosque, Jakarta, the national mosque of Indonesia, since the mosque was the gift from Indonesian people and government for Bosnia and Herzegovina as a token of solidarity and friendship between two nations.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Indonesia">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Indonesia is a country whose national mosque is Istiklal Mosque, Jakarta.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was named after Istiqlal Mosque, Jakarta, the national mosque of Indonesia, since the mosque was the gift from Indonesian people and government for Bosnia and Herzegovina as a token of solidarity and friendship between two nations.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Indonesian people and government">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Indonesian people and government gifted the Istiklal Mosque in Sarajevo as a token of solidarity and friendship.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was named after Istiqlal Mosque, Jakarta, the national mosque of Indonesia, since the mosque was the gift from Indonesian people and government for Bosnia and Herzegovina as a token of solidarity and friendship between two nations.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="istiqlal">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Arabic word 'istiqlal' means 'independence'.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The name \"istiqlal\" is Arabic word for \"independence\", thus it is also meant to commemorate the independence of Bosnia and Herzegovina.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="independence">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Independence is the meaning of the Arabic word 'istiqlal' and is commemorated by the Istiklal Mosque in Sarajevo.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The name \"istiqlal\" is Arabic word for \"independence\", thus it is also meant to commemorate the independence of Bosnia and Herzegovina.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Indonesian mosque">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Indonesian mosque is a colloquial name for Istiklal Mosque in Sarajevo.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It is also colloquially known as \"Indonesian mosque\" or \"Suharto mosque\", as the credit to the initiator of the mosque construction.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Suharto mosque">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Suharto mosque is a colloquial name for Istiklal Mosque in Sarajevo, given credit to the initiator of its construction.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It is also colloquially known as \"Indonesian mosque\" or \"Suharto mosque\", as the credit to the initiator of the mosque construction.", "chunk_id": "1cbf4009842f5c2b", "doc_id": "hp_1228d503d45e5b99"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1228d503d45e5b99"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Slip of the Lip">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A song recorded by the American heavy metal band Ratt in 1986.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Slip of the Lip\" is a song recorded by American heavy metal band Ratt in 1986 from the album \"Dancing Undercover\" and is also on their greatest hits album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Ratt">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "Ratt is a heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "a5a1a3ab9a01f805", "doc_id": "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462"}, {"text": "Ratt is an American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "An American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "An American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}, {"text": "Ratt was a band that Stephen Pearcy was associated with.", "chunk_id": "8efa335de18f3d03", "doc_id": "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1"}, {"text": "An American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "An American heavy metal band.", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}, {"text": "A glam metal band in which Matt Thorr played bass.", "chunk_id": "69ea2310084e6973", "doc_id": "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Slip of the Lip\" is a song recorded by American heavy metal band Ratt in 1986 from the album \"Dancing Undercover\" and is also on their greatest hits album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "He is best known as the founder, singer, and songwriter of the heavy metal band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "a5a1a3ab9a01f805", "doc_id": "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462"}, {"text": "\"Eat Me Up Alive\" is a song by American heavy metal band Ratt, the song was the second single off the bands 2010 album Infestation.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "\"Lovin' You's a Dirty Job\" is a single by the American heavy metal band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "\"Wanted Man' is the first track on American heavy metal band Ratt's album \"Out of the Cellar\".", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}, {"text": "Stephen Pearcy was said to still be in his Ratt form, indeed his voice was just as compelling.", "chunk_id": "8efa335de18f3d03", "doc_id": "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1"}, {"text": "\"Dance\" is a song by American heavy metal band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "\"Shame Shame Shame\" is a single by American heavy metal band Ratt, released on their album \"Detonator\".", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}, {"text": "Matt Thorne, also known as Matt Thorr, is a bassist who has played in the glam metal band Ratt, Rough Cutt, and Jailhouse.", "chunk_id": "69ea2310084e6973", "doc_id": "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462", "hp_502c6729d1d13614", "hp_58add9e266f469f0", "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32", "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1", "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e", "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e", "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Dancing Undercover">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An album by the band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "The 1986 album by Ratt from which the song 'Dance' is the first track.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Slip of the Lip\" is a song recorded by American heavy metal band Ratt in 1986 from the album \"Dancing Undercover\" and is also on their greatest hits album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "It is the first track off their 1986 album \"Dancing Undercover\" and the tenth track of their compilation album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A greatest hits album by the band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "A compilation album by Ratt.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Slip of the Lip\" is a song recorded by American heavy metal band Ratt in 1986 from the album \"Dancing Undercover\" and is also on their greatest hits album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "It is the first track off their 1986 album \"Dancing Undercover\" and the tenth track of their compilation album \"Ratt &amp; Roll 81-91\".", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Stephen Pearcy">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Vocalist for the band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "Stephen Pearcy is an American musician.", "chunk_id": "a5a1a3ab9a01f805", "doc_id": "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462"}, {"text": "Stephen Pearcy is a songwriter for the song Eat Me Up Alive.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "Lead singer of Ratt and co-writer of the song 'Lovin' You's a Dirty Job'.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "A composer of the song \"Wanted Man\".", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}, {"text": "Stephen Pearcy was a member of the alternative metal band Vicious Delite.", "chunk_id": "8efa335de18f3d03", "doc_id": "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1"}, {"text": "A songwriter for the song 'Dance'.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "Lead singer of Ratt who co-wrote the song \"Shame Shame Shame\".", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}, {"text": "A person who co-wrote the song \"Back For More\" with Matt Thorr.", "chunk_id": "69ea2310084e6973", "doc_id": "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was written by Ratt vocalist Stephen Pearcy, bassist Juan Croucier, and guitarist Warren DeMartini.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "Stephen Eric Pearcy (born July 3, 1956), known as Stephen Pearcy, is an American musician.", "chunk_id": "a5a1a3ab9a01f805", "doc_id": "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462"}, {"text": "The song was written by Carlos Cavazo, Warren Demartini, and Stephen Pearcy for the team to have the song sound like it came out of 1984.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "The song was co-written by Ratt bassist Juan Croucier, guitarist Warren DeMartini, lead singer Stephen Pearcy and songwriter Desmond Child.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "The song was composed by Robbin Crosby, Stephen Pearcy, and Joey Cristofanilli (who was briefly filling in for full-time Ratt bassist Juan Croucier), and it was the second biggest hit on the album (note: \"Back for more\" did not qualify for a chart position since it was not an actual single release), reaching number 87 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 and 38 on the \"Billboard\" Mainstream Rock Tracks chart.", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}, {"text": "Vicious Delite was an alternative metal band formed by Stephen Pearcy in 1995 after Arcade.", "chunk_id": "8efa335de18f3d03", "doc_id": "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1"}, {"text": "The song was written by Stephen Pearcy, guitarists Robbin Crosby and Warren DeMartini, and album producer Beau Hill.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "The song was co-written by famed songwriter Desmond Child, Ratt guitarist Warren DeMartini and lead singer Stephen Pearcy.", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}, {"text": "He co-wrote the song \"Back For More\" with Stephen Pearcy on Ratt's album \"Out of the Cellar\".", "chunk_id": "69ea2310084e6973", "doc_id": "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_2a3d8bb73905c462", "hp_502c6729d1d13614", "hp_58add9e266f469f0", "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32", "hp_810b9dfd1db699c1", "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e", "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e", "hp_b786bea89d57e8fa"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Juan Croucier">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Bassist for the band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "Ratt bassist and co-writer of the song 'Lovin' You's a Dirty Job'.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "The full-time bassist for Ratt.", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was written by Ratt vocalist Stephen Pearcy, bassist Juan Croucier, and guitarist Warren DeMartini.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "The song was co-written by Ratt bassist Juan Croucier, guitarist Warren DeMartini, lead singer Stephen Pearcy and songwriter Desmond Child.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "The song was composed by Robbin Crosby, Stephen Pearcy, and Joey Cristofanilli (who was briefly filling in for full-time Ratt bassist Juan Croucier), and it was the second biggest hit on the album (note: \"Back for more\" did not qualify for a chart position since it was not an actual single release), reaching number 87 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 and 38 on the \"Billboard\" Mainstream Rock Tracks chart.", "chunk_id": "1c9e0b6104c2f63d", "doc_id": "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_58add9e266f469f0", "hp_7dbc7d21f7c51f32"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Warren DeMartini">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Guitarist for the band Ratt.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "Warren Demartini is a songwriter for the song Eat Me Up Alive.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "Guitarist for Ratt and co-writer of the song 'Lovin' You's a Dirty Job'.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "A guitarist and songwriter for the song 'Dance'.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "Ratt guitarist who co-wrote the song \"Shame Shame Shame\".", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It was written by Ratt vocalist Stephen Pearcy, bassist Juan Croucier, and guitarist Warren DeMartini.", "chunk_id": "4e0b38de0e3e7000", "doc_id": "hp_12b49066ad532acc"}, {"text": "The song was written by Carlos Cavazo, Warren Demartini, and Stephen Pearcy for the team to have the song sound like it came out of 1984.", "chunk_id": "bb6e05f103a87cac", "doc_id": "hp_502c6729d1d13614"}, {"text": "The song was co-written by Ratt bassist Juan Croucier, guitarist Warren DeMartini, lead singer Stephen Pearcy and songwriter Desmond Child.", "chunk_id": "66ad2e860f938fc7", "doc_id": "hp_58add9e266f469f0"}, {"text": "The song was written by Stephen Pearcy, guitarists Robbin Crosby and Warren DeMartini, and album producer Beau Hill.", "chunk_id": "73789dfc1fb7d82f", "doc_id": "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e"}, {"text": "The song was co-written by famed songwriter Desmond Child, Ratt guitarist Warren DeMartini and lead singer Stephen Pearcy.", "chunk_id": "6320f732c14ef885", "doc_id": "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_12b49066ad532acc", "hp_502c6729d1d13614", "hp_58add9e266f469f0", "hp_8e4dbca3117ac40e", "hp_95b0346082b5ad8e"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Paul Sloane">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Paul Sloane was a writer and film director.", "chunk_id": "0e4827edb0f6cbcd", "doc_id": "hp_1302f518109754f9"}, {"text": "Director and writer of the film Here Comes the Band.", "chunk_id": "57471210b669a2c2", "doc_id": "hp_3098272d755d2c40"}, {"text": "An author of lateral thinking puzzle books.", "chunk_id": "a5fa71ec4370bfe1", "doc_id": "hp_94fc8885003296ef"}, {"text": "Paul Sloane was the director and writer of the film Lone Cowboy.", "chunk_id": "380a38a9ac47b4e3", "doc_id": "hp_9cc049995d15cd22"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Paul Sloane (April 19, 1893 – November 15, 1963) was a writer and film director who directed 26 films from 1925 to 1952, and wrote or co-wrote 35 films.", "chunk_id": "0e4827edb0f6cbcd", "doc_id": "hp_1302f518109754f9"}, {"text": "Here Comes the Band is a 1935 American comedy film directed by Paul Sloane and written by Paul Sloane, Ralph Spence and Victor Mansfield.", "chunk_id": "57471210b669a2c2", "doc_id": "hp_3098272d755d2c40"}, {"text": "Surōn to Makuhēru no Nazo no Sutōrī (スローンとマクヘールの謎の物語 , \"Paul Sloane and Des MacHale's Intriguing Tales\") is based on the concept of lateral thinking puzzles, books authored by Paul Sloane and Des MacHale.", "chunk_id": "a5fa71ec4370bfe1", "doc_id": "hp_94fc8885003296ef"}, {"text": "Lone Cowboy is a 1933 American Pre-Code Western film directed by Paul Sloane and written by Paul Sloane, Agnes Brand Leahy, Bobby Vernon and Will James.", "chunk_id": "380a38a9ac47b4e3", "doc_id": "hp_9cc049995d15cd22"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1302f518109754f9", "hp_3098272d755d2c40", "hp_94fc8885003296ef", "hp_9cc049995d15cd22"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Hearts in Dixie">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Hearts in Dixie is a film directed by Paul Sloane.", "chunk_id": "0e4827edb0f6cbcd", "doc_id": "hp_1302f518109754f9"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "His movies include \"Hearts in Dixie\" (1929) with Stepin Fetchit, \"The Woman Accused\" (1933) with Cary Grant, and \"The Texans\" with Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott and Walter Brennan.", "chunk_id": "0e4827edb0f6cbcd", "doc_id": "hp_1302f518109754f9"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_1302f518109754f9"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
