<node id="Bernhard Cossmann">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Bernhard Cossmann was a German cellist.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Bernhard Cossmann (17 May 1822 – 7 May 1910) was a German cellist.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Dessau">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Dessau is the birthplace of Bernhard Cossmann.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Born in Dessau, he first studied under Theodore Muller.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Theodore Muller">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Theodore Muller was a teacher of Bernhard Cossmann.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Born in Dessau, he first studied under Theodore Muller.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Grand Opera in Paris">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Grand Opera in Paris is an institution where Bernhard Cossmann worked.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "During his life, he worked for the Grand Opera in Paris and became acquainted with Franz Liszt, with whom he went to Weimar.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Franz Liszt">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Franz Liszt was a person Bernhard Cossmann became acquainted with.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "During his life, he worked for the Grand Opera in Paris and became acquainted with Franz Liszt, with whom he went to Weimar.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Weimar">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Weimar is a location Bernhard Cossmann went to with Franz Liszt.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "During his life, he worked for the Grand Opera in Paris and became acquainted with Franz Liszt, with whom he went to Weimar.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Moscow Conservatory">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Moscow Conservatory is an institution where Cossmann was appointed professor of cello studies.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "In 1866, Cossmann was appointed professor of cello studies at the Moscow Conservatory.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Frankfurt">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Frankfurt is the city where the Hoch Conservatory was founded.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}, {"text": "Frankfurt is a location in Germany where the Hoch Conservatory Foundation was founded.", "chunk_id": "43c8b1224e31b404", "doc_id": "hp_a82ec9e667a313c7"}, {"text": "Frankfurt is a location where the Hoch Conservatory is situated and where Alma Moodie became a teacher.", "chunk_id": "5978fb5d2c7a87fb", "doc_id": "hp_ac759163e3bcf4aa"}, {"text": "Frankfurt is a city where Iwan Knorr settled and joined the faculty of the Hoch Conservatory, and where he later died.", "chunk_id": "902ae288e12f1dfb", "doc_id": "hp_d0d1ade1227af443"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "However, in 1878, Cossmann helped found the Hoch Conservatory in Frankfurt, where he occupied the post of teacher of cello.", "chunk_id": "6edb4fdb819c98a3", "doc_id": "hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd"}, {"text": "He willed his fortune to the Hoch Conservatory Foundation, founded in 1878 in Frankfurt.", "chunk_id": "43c8b1224e31b404", "doc_id": "hp_a82ec9e667a313c7"}, {"text": "She became a teacher at the Hoch Conservatory in Frankfurt.", "chunk_id": "5978fb5d2c7a87fb", "doc_id": "hp_ac759163e3bcf4aa"}, {"text": "In 1883 he settled in Frankfurt, where he joined the faculty of the Hoch Conservatory; in 1908 he became director of the school.", "chunk_id": "902ae288e12f1dfb", "doc_id": "hp_d0d1ade1227af443"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_67a26b8d9bf904cd", "hp_a82ec9e667a313c7", "hp_ac759163e3bcf4aa", "hp_d0d1ade1227af443"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Shin Sang-ok">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A prolific South Korean film producer and director.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}, {"text": "Director of the 1985 North Korean film Pulgasari.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}, {"text": "A director who was kidnapped in 1978 by North Korean intelligence.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Shin Sang-ok (October 11, 1926 – April 11, 2006) was a prolific South Korean film producer and director with more than 100 producer and 70 director credits to his name.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}, {"text": "Pulgasari (Chosŏn'gŭl: 불가사리 ; RR: \"Bulgasari \" ) is a 1985 North Korean dark fantasy-action monster film directed by Shin Sang-ok and Chong Gon Jo.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}, {"text": "Director Shin had been kidnapped in 1978 by North Korean intelligence on the orders of Kim Jong-il, son of the then-ruling Kim Il-sung.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_68770e756b7fedd1", "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="South Korean">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Refers to the country of South Korea.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Shin Sang-ok (October 11, 1926 – April 11, 2006) was a prolific South Korean film producer and director with more than 100 producer and 70 director credits to his name.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_68770e756b7fedd1"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Prince of Korean Cinema">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A moniker given to Shin Sang-ok during the 1950s and 60s.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "His best-known films were made in the 1950s and 60s when he was known as the \"Prince of Korean Cinema\".", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_68770e756b7fedd1"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Gold Crown Cultural Medal">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The country's top honor for an artist in South Korea.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "He received the Gold Crown Cultural Medal, the country's top honor for an artist.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_68770e756b7fedd1"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Kim Jong-il">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The previous North Korean leader who kidnapped Shin Sang-ok.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}, {"text": "Ordered the kidnapping of Shin Sang-ok in 1978 and was the son of Kim Il-sung.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}, {"text": "The individual who personally chose the title for the film series and was extensively involved in its early episodes.", "chunk_id": "459ce873024f40a4", "doc_id": "hp_d64aea307fb47882"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "He is also known for having been kidnapped by the previous North Korean leader, Kim Jong-il, for the purpose of producing critically acclaimed films.", "chunk_id": "8b133d46fe97fa0d", "doc_id": "hp_68770e756b7fedd1"}, {"text": "Director Shin had been kidnapped in 1978 by North Korean intelligence on the orders of Kim Jong-il, son of the then-ruling Kim Il-sung.", "chunk_id": "f5d5ccb7eff4c8c9", "doc_id": "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0"}, {"text": "Kim Jong-Il personally chose the title and was extensively involved in the early episodes.", "chunk_id": "459ce873024f40a4", "doc_id": "hp_d64aea307fb47882"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_68770e756b7fedd1", "hp_b6688ab9041dc4a0", "hp_d64aea307fb47882"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
