<node id="14:59">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The third studio album by American rock band Sugar Ray.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "14:59 is the third studio album by American rock band Sugar Ray, released on January 12, 1999.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_767039256bd5743c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="RIAA">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Recording Industry Association of America, which certifies music releases.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}, {"text": "The Recording Industry Association of America, which certified the album Black Gives Way to Blue as gold.", "chunk_id": "a02521ae2928a8dd", "doc_id": "hp_7e9f73906e63aaa8"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It entered the top 20 on the \"Billboard\" 200, peaking at number 17&lt;ref name=\"1459/billboard\"&gt; &lt;/ref&gt; and certified triple-platinum by the RIAA.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}, {"text": "The album debuted at No. 5 on the \"Billboard\" 200, and was certified gold by the RIAA on May 26, 2010, with shipments exceeding 500,000 copies and has sold 1 million copies worldwide.", "chunk_id": "a02521ae2928a8dd", "doc_id": "hp_7e9f73906e63aaa8"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_767039256bd5743c", "hp_7e9f73906e63aaa8"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="nu metal">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A genre of music that combines elements of heavy metal with other genres such as hip hop, alternative rock, and funk.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The album shows the band moving into a more mainstream pop rock sound, away from their earlier funk metal and nu metal sound, due to the success of their single \"Fly\" off their prior album, \"Floored\".", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_767039256bd5743c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="15 minutes of fame">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A concept referring to a brief period of widespread attention or popularity.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The album's title is a self-deprecating reference to the \"15 minutes of fame\" critics claimed the band was riding on.", "chunk_id": "a83c783458cb120f", "doc_id": "hp_767039256bd5743c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_767039256bd5743c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Zilch memo">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A memorandum known as the Zilch memo was an American government document sent by National Security Advisor Henry Kissinger to President Richard Nixon on January 3, 1972, about the military situation in Laos during the Vietnam War.", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "A memorandum known as the Zilch memo was an American government document sent by National Security Advisor Henry Kissinger to President Richard Nixon on January 3, 1972, about the military situation in Laos during the Vietnam War.", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Henry Kissinger">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "National Security Advisor who sent the Zilch memo.", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "A memorandum known as the Zilch memo was an American government document sent by National Security Advisor Henry Kissinger to President Richard Nixon on January 3, 1972, about the military situation in Laos during the Vietnam War.", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50"]</data>
