<node id="Mircea David">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Mircea David was a Romanian football player who played as a goalkeeper.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Mircea David (16 October 1914 – 12 October 1993) was a Romanian football player, who played as a goalkeeper.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_768bd041b089468b"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Romania">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Romania is a country in Europe.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Mircea David (16 October 1914 – 12 October 1993) was a Romanian football player, who played as a goalkeeper.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_768bd041b089468b"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Il Dio">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "\"Il Dio\" is a nickname given to Mircea David by Italian football fans.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "After a match between Romania and Italy, played in Rome, he was nicknamed by the Italian football fans \"Il Dio\", because of his incredible saves.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_768bd041b089468b"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Romania national football team">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Romania national football team is the national football team of Romania.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "He was a member of the Romania national football team which competed at the 1938 FIFA World Cup, but did not play any match.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_768bd041b089468b"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="1938 FIFA World Cup">
  <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The 1938 FIFA World Cup was the third FIFA World Cup, the interland football tournament contested by the men's national teams of the member associations of FIFA.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "He was a member of the Romania national football team which competed at the 1938 FIFA World Cup, but did not play any match.", "chunk_id": "7774f7d0fd5a7c20", "doc_id": "hp_768bd041b089468b"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_768bd041b089468b"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Wild Mountain Thyme">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A Scottish folk song also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "A melody on which \"A Pair of Brown Eyes\" was composed.", "chunk_id": "b52105e0cc7ef21b", "doc_id": "hp_da38acdde66d2037"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Wild Mountain Thyme\" (also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\") is a Scottish folk song that was collected by Francis McPeake 1st, who wrote the song himself for his wife.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "It featured on the band's second album, \"Rum Sodomy &amp; the Lash\", and was composed by Pogues front man Shane MacGowan, on the melody of \"Wild Mountain Thyme\", also known as \"Will Ye Go Lassie Go,\" a song by Francis McPeake in a traditional Irish folk style.", "chunk_id": "b52105e0cc7ef21b", "doc_id": "hp_da38acdde66d2037"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514", "hp_da38acdde66d2037"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Purple Heather">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An alternative title for the Scottish folk song \"Wild Mountain Thyme\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Wild Mountain Thyme\" (also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\") is a Scottish folk song that was collected by Francis McPeake 1st, who wrote the song himself for his wife.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Francis McPeake 1st">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The collector and writer of the Scottish folk song \"Wild Mountain Thyme\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Wild Mountain Thyme\" (also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\") is a Scottish folk song that was collected by Francis McPeake 1st, who wrote the song himself for his wife.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="McPeake family">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A musical family who claim recognition for the writing of the song \"Wild Mountain Thyme\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The McPeake family claim recognition for the writing of the song.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Belfast">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A city in Northern Ireland where Francis McPeake is a member of a well-known musical family.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "A city in Northern Ireland where the River Farset is located.", "chunk_id": "ffad64e807ea3b1b", "doc_id": "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Francis McPeake is a member of a well known musical family in Belfast, Northern Ireland.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "The River Farset (\"An Fhearsaid\" or \"Abhainn na Feirste\" in Irish) is a river in Belfast, Northern Ireland, a tributary of the River Lagan.", "chunk_id": "ffad64e807ea3b1b", "doc_id": "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514", "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Northern Ireland">
  <data key="d0">LOCATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A country where Francis McPeake's musical family is well-known.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "A country that is part of the United Kingdom, where Belfast and the River Farset are located.", "chunk_id": "ffad64e807ea3b1b", "doc_id": "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Francis McPeake is a member of a well known musical family in Belfast, Northern Ireland.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}, {"text": "The River Farset (\"An Fhearsaid\" or \"Abhainn na Feirste\" in Irish) is a river in Belfast, Northern Ireland, a tributary of the River Lagan.", "chunk_id": "ffad64e807ea3b1b", "doc_id": "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514", "hp_cc911f866aa0efaa"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="The Braes of Balquhither">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A song by Scottish poet Robert Tannahill, whose lyrics and melody are a variant of \"Wild Mountain Thyme\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The lyrics and melody are a variant of the song \"The Braes of Balquhither\" by Scottish poet Robert Tannahill (1774–1810), a contemporary of Robert Burns.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Robert Tannahill">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A Scottish poet who wrote \"The Braes of Balquhither\".", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The lyrics and melody are a variant of the song \"The Braes of Balquhither\" by Scottish poet Robert Tannahill (1774–1810), a contemporary of Robert Burns.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Robert Burns">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A contemporary of Robert Tannahill.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The lyrics and melody are a variant of the song \"The Braes of Balquhither\" by Scottish poet Robert Tannahill (1774–1810), a contemporary of Robert Burns.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Scottish Minstrel">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A publication from 1821-24 where Robert Tannahill's original song was first published.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Tannahill's original song, first published in Robert Archibald Smith's \"Scottish Minstrel\" (1821–24), is about the hills (\"braes\") around Balquhidder near Lochearnhead.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Robert Archibald Smith">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The publisher of \"Scottish Minstrel\" where Tannahill's song was first published.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Tannahill's original song, first published in Robert Archibald Smith's \"Scottish Minstrel\" (1821–24), is about the hills (\"braes\") around Balquhidder near Lochearnhead.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_7778e88209c46514"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
