<node id="Carlo Innocenzo Frugoni">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The translator of the libretto for 'Castore e Polluce'.", "chunk_id": "b9a0568248760524", "doc_id": "hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The libretto was one translated by Carlo Innocenzo Frugoni, from Pierre-Joseph-Justin Bernard's French text for Rameau's \"Castor et Pollux\".", "chunk_id": "b9a0568248760524", "doc_id": "hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Pierre-Joseph-Justin Bernard">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The author of the original French text for Rameau's 'Castor et Pollux'.", "chunk_id": "b9a0568248760524", "doc_id": "hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The libretto was one translated by Carlo Innocenzo Frugoni, from Pierre-Joseph-Justin Bernard's French text for Rameau's \"Castor et Pollux\".", "chunk_id": "b9a0568248760524", "doc_id": "hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9ebf1cff727fec5"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="solo prepared piano">
  <data key="d0">TECHNOLOGY</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A prepared piano intended to be played by a single performer.", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "American avant-garde composer John Cage (1912–1992) started composing pieces for solo prepared piano around 1938–40.", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9f272e0c789745f"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Merce Cunningham">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A frequent collaborator with John Cage, for whom Cage created music to accompany dances.", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The majority of early works for this instrument were created to accompany dances by Cage's various collaborators, most frequently Merce Cunningham.", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9f272e0c789745f"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Henry Cowell">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A predecessor cited by John Cage in response to criticisms of prepared piano.", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "In response to frequent criticisms of prepared piano, Cage cited numerous predecessors (such as Henry Cowell).", "chunk_id": "b7f8e7da447a5f1c", "doc_id": "hp_e9f272e0c789745f"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9f272e0c789745f"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Restless (Carl Perkins song)">
  <data key="d0">SONG</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A 1968 song written by Carl Perkins.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Restless\" is a 1968 song written by Carl Perkins and released as a single on Columbia Records.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Carl Perkins">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The writer and performer of the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Restless\" is a 1968 song written by Carl Perkins and released as a single on Columbia Records.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Columbia Records">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The record label that released the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "\"Restless\" is a 1968 song written by Carl Perkins and released as a single on Columbia Records.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Billboard country chart">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A music chart that ranked the song 'Restless' at number 20.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "..., reaching no. 20 on the \"Billboard\" country chart.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Bill Denny">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "One of the producers of the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The recording, produced by Bill Denny and Larry Butler, also appeared on the May, 1969 Columbia LP \"Carl Perkins' Greatest Hits\".", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Larry Butler">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "One of the producers of the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The recording, produced by Bill Denny and Larry Butler, also appeared on the May, 1969 Columbia LP \"Carl Perkins' Greatest Hits\".", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Carl Perkins' Greatest Hits">
  <data key="d0">ALBUM</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A Columbia LP released in May 1969 that included the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The recording, produced by Bill Denny and Larry Butler, also appeared on the May, 1969 Columbia LP \"Carl Perkins' Greatest Hits\".", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Restless: The Columbia Recordings">
  <data key="d0">ALBUM</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A 1992 Carl Perkins compilation album that included the song 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The song also appeared on the 1992 Carl Perkins compilation album \"Restless: The Columbia Recordings\".", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Mark O'Connor">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An artist who recorded a new version of 'Restless' in 1991.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}, {"text": "Mark O'Connor is an American violinist, fiddler, composer, and music teacher known for his work in bluegrass, jazz, and country music.", "chunk_id": "83ff2598ffe55600", "doc_id": "hp_f2e2e7c2a56a9856"}, {"text": "Mark O'Connor is a musician who released the country album 'The New Nashville Cats'.", "chunk_id": "a099b3930b3ba4f5", "doc_id": "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The song became a major hit again in 1991 in a new all-star recording by Mark O'Connor and The New Nashville Cats.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}, {"text": "Mark O'Connor (born August 5, 1961, Seattle) is an American bluegrass, jazz and country violinist, fiddler, composer and music teacher.", "chunk_id": "83ff2598ffe55600", "doc_id": "hp_f2e2e7c2a56a9856"}, {"text": "The New Nashville Cats is a country album by Mark O'Connor, in conjunction with a variety of other musical artists.", "chunk_id": "a099b3930b3ba4f5", "doc_id": "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7", "hp_f2e2e7c2a56a9856", "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="The New Nashville Cats">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A group that recorded a new version of 'Restless' with Mark O'Connor in 1991.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}, {"text": "The New Nashville Cats is a country album by Mark O'Connor.", "chunk_id": "a099b3930b3ba4f5", "doc_id": "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The song became a major hit again in 1991 in a new all-star recording by Mark O'Connor and The New Nashville Cats.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}, {"text": "The New Nashville Cats is a country album by Mark O'Connor, in conjunction with a variety of other musical artists.", "chunk_id": "a099b3930b3ba4f5", "doc_id": "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7", "hp_fa69475a7ed355e8"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Kraft Music Hall">
  <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A television show where Carl Perkins performed 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Carl Perkins performed the song on the Kraft Music Hall episode hosted by Johnny Cash on April 16, 1969.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Johnny Cash">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The host of the Kraft Music Hall episode where Carl Perkins performed 'Restless'.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Carl Perkins performed the song on the Kraft Music Hall episode hosted by Johnny Cash on April 16, 1969.", "chunk_id": "dd57b3e345e8ca53", "doc_id": "hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_e9fa02b2953b7da7"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Holocaust">
  <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A genocide in which approximately six million European Jews were killed by Nazi Germany and its World War II collaborators.", "chunk_id": "b24df285a9cc0262", "doc_id": "hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "A timeline of the Holocaust is detailed in the events listed below.", "chunk_id": "b24df285a9cc0262", "doc_id": "hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="United States Holocaust Memorial Museum">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A museum and memorial in Washington, D.C., dedicated to preserving the memory of the Holocaust.", "chunk_id": "b24df285a9cc0262", "doc_id": "hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The following timeline has been compiled from a variety of sources including the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum.", "chunk_id": "b24df285a9cc0262", "doc_id": "hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea0eba1a0f59fdf8"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Rootare–Prenzlow equation">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper, \"Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements,\" Rootare, H.M., and Prenzlow, C.F., 71 J. Phys. Chem. p. 2733 (1967).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Hillar Rootare">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An Estonian-American scientist for whom the Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper, \"Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements,\" Rootare, H.M., and Prenzlow, C.F., 71 J. Phys. Chem. p. 2733 (1967).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Carl Prenzlow">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "An American scientist for whom the Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper, \"Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements,\" Rootare, H.M., and Prenzlow, C.F., 71 J. Phys. Chem. p. 2733 (1967).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The title of the 1967 paper published by Hillar Rootare and Carl Prenzlow.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper, \"Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements,\" Rootare, H.M., and Prenzlow, C.F., 71 J. Phys. Chem. p. 2733 (1967).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="J. Phys. Chem.">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Abbreviation for the Journal of Physical Chemistry, where the 1967 paper was published.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The Rootare-Prenzlow Equation is named for Estonian-American scientist Hillar Rootare and American scientist Carl Prenzlow, first published in their 1967 paper, \"Surface Areas from Mercury Porosimetry Measurements,\" Rootare, H.M., and Prenzlow, C.F., 71 J. Phys. Chem. p. 2733 (1967).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="mercury porosimetry">
  <data key="d0">TECHNOLOGY</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A testing method used to determine the pore size distribution of porous materials.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "The equation first formulated a means to calculate cumulative surface areas of porous solids based on data taken in mercury porosimetry testing.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="A Computer Program for Pore Volume and Pore Area Distribution">
  <data key="d0">OTHER</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "The title of a computer program devised by Rootare and Spencer for automated calculations.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Rootare and Spencer later devised a computer programme to carry out automated calculations, \"A Computer Program for Pore Volume and Pore Area Distribution,\" Rootare &amp; Spencer, Perspectives in Powder Metallurgy (Advanced Experimental Techniques in Powder Metallurgy) p. 225, Plenum Press (New York, London 1970).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Spencer">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A scientist who, along with Rootare, devised a computer program for automated calculations.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Rootare and Spencer later devised a computer programme to carry out automated calculations, \"A Computer Program for Pore Volume and Pore Area Distribution,\" Rootare &amp; Spencer, Perspectives in Powder Metallurgy (Advanced Experimental Techniques in Powder Metallurgy) p. 225, Plenum Press (New York, London 1970).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Plenum Press">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "A publisher of the book 'Perspectives in Powder Metallurgy'.", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Rootare and Spencer later devised a computer programme to carry out automated calculations, \"A Computer Program for Pore Volume and Pore Area Distribution,\" Rootare &amp; Spencer, Perspectives in Powder Metallurgy (Advanced Experimental Techniques in Powder Metallurgy) p. 225, Plenum Press (New York, London 1970).", "chunk_id": "0f6cd2c39ceebefb", "doc_id": "hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea52b11b7f3d5b7c"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Mentheae">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Mentheae is a tribe within the Lamiaceae family.", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Within the Lamiaceae, \"Salvia\" is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae.", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="Nepetoideae">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Nepetoideae is a subfamily within the Lamiaceae family.", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "Within the Lamiaceae, \"Salvia\" is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae.", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
<node id="sage">
  <data key="d0">CONCEPT</data>
  <data key="d1">[{"text": "Sage is a common name for several genera of plants, including Salvia.", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}, {"text": "Sages is a common name for some species within the Leucophyllum genus.", "chunk_id": "d3f92c958cb5561c", "doc_id": "hp_51931a701df55281"}, {"text": "Sages are a type of flowering plant in the genus Salvia.", "chunk_id": "993393a5d9cfab3f", "doc_id": "hp_bae29dd7ee1c4c2e"}]</data>
  <data key="d2">[{"text": "It is one of several genera commonly referred to as sage, It includes the widely produced herb used in cooking, \"Salvia officinalis\" (common sage, or just \"sage\").", "chunk_id": "5634c2e12f5049d1", "doc_id": "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"}, {"text": "The dozen-odd species are often called \"sages\", although they have no relationship to the genus \"Salvia\".", "chunk_id": "d3f92c958cb5561c", "doc_id": "hp_51931a701df55281"}, {"text": "Salvia chamelaeagnea is a species of flowering plant in genus \"Salvia\", known as sages.", "chunk_id": "993393a5d9cfab3f", "doc_id": "hp_bae29dd7ee1c4c2e"}]</data>
  <data key="d3">["hp_51931a701df55281", "hp_bae29dd7ee1c4c2e", "hp_ea708a0e606fa04d"]</data>
  <data key="d4">False</data>
</node>
