<edge source="Hazel Dickens" target="Alice Gerrard">
  <data key="d5">["COLLABORATED_WITH"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Hazel Dickens and Alice Gerrard were pioneers in recording a bluegrass album together.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "With Alice Gerrard, Dickens was one of the first women to record a bluegrass album.", "chunk_id": "12b5fe23f857cd32", "doc_id": "hp_2295401c0eccc650", "type": "COLLABORATED_WITH"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2295401c0eccc650</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hazel Dickens" target="John Pietaro">
  <data key="d5">["MENTIONED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">John Pietaro commented on Hazel Dickens's life and music.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Cultural blogger John Pietaro noted that \"Dickens didn’t just sing the anthems of labor, she lived them and her place on many a picket line, staring down gunfire and goon squads, embedded her into the cause.\"", "chunk_id": "12b5fe23f857cd32", "doc_id": "hp_2295401c0eccc650", "type": "MENTIONED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2295401c0eccc650</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hazel Dickens" target="bluegrass music">
  <data key="d5">["PIONEER_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Hazel Dickens was a pioneer among women in bluegrass music.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "\"The New York Times\" extolled her as \"a clarion-voiced advocate for coal miners and working people and a pioneer among women in bluegrass music.\"", "chunk_id": "12b5fe23f857cd32", "doc_id": "hp_2295401c0eccc650", "type": "PIONEER_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2295401c0eccc650</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Hazel Dickens" target="The &quot;New York Times&quot;">
  <data key="d5">["PRAISED_BY"]</data>
