<edge source="Shumsher" target="Singha Durbar">
  <data key="d5">["BUILT"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Shumsher built Singha Durbar.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It was built by Shumsher immediately after accession to the post of Prime Minister.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "BUILT"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shumsher" target="Government of Nepal">
  <data key="d5">["SOLD"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Shumsher sold Singha Durbar to the Government of Nepal.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Immediately after construction Shumsher sold this property to the Government of Nepal for 20 million Nepali rupee as the official residence of Prime minister.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "SOLD"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Government of Nepal" target="Singha Durbar">
  <data key="d5">["OFFICIAL_RESIDENCE_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Singha Durbar was the official residence of the Prime Minister for the Government of Nepal.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Immediately after construction Shumsher sold this property to the Government of Nepal for 20 million Nepali rupee as the official residence of Prime minister.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "OFFICIAL_RESIDENCE_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Padma Shumsher JBR" target="Bishalnagar Durbar">
  <data key="d5">["LIVED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Padma Shumsher JBR lived in Bishalnagar Durbar.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The last Rana Prime minister to occupy Singha Durbar was Mohan Shumsher JBR.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "LIVED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Mohan Shumsher JBR" target="Singha Durbar">
  <data key="d5">["OCCUPIED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Mohan Shumsher JBR was the last Rana Prime Minister to occupy Singha Durbar.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The last Rana Prime minister to occupy Singha Durbar was Mohan Shumsher JBR.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "OCCUPIED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Rana Dynasty" target="Singha Durbar">
  <data key="d5">["OFFICIAL_RESIDENCE_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Singha Durbar was the official residence of prime ministers of the Rana Dynasty.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "After his death in 1929, it was used as the official residence of prime ministers of Rana dynasty except Padma Shumsher JBR, who lived in his own Bishalnagar Durbar.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "OFFICIAL_RESIDENCE_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="His Majesty's Government" target="Mohan Shumsher JBR">
  <data key="d5">["ORDERED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">His Majesty's Government ordered Mohan Shumsher JBR to leave the palace.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Even after the fall of Rana Dynasty in 1951, Mohan used this place, but in 1953 he was ordered by His Majesty's Government to leave the palace which became a National Property.", "chunk_id": "d825b9ccce2e950e", "doc_id": "hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b", "type": "ORDERED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2bdac4251efc5b9b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="United States v. Nixon" target="United States Supreme Court">
  <data key="d5">["DECIDED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The United States Supreme Court made the decision in the case of United States v. Nixon.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "United States v. Nixon, 418 U.S. 683 (1974), was a landmark United States Supreme Court decision which resulted in a unanimous 8–0 ruling against President Richard Nixon, ordering him to deliver presidential tape recordings and other subpoenaed materials to the District Court.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "DECIDED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec</data>
</edge>
<edge source="United States v. Nixon" target="Richard Nixon">
  <data key="d5">["RULING_AGAINST", "IMPORTANT_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court ruled against President Richard Nixon in the United States v. Nixon case.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "United States v. Nixon, 418 U.S. 683 (1974), was a landmark United States Supreme Court decision which resulted in a unanimous 8–0 ruling against President Richard Nixon, ordering him to deliver presidential tape recordings and other subpoenaed materials to the District Court.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "RULING_AGAINST"}, {"text": "Issued on July 24, 1974, the ruling was important to the late stages of the Watergate scandal, when there was an ongoing impeachment process against Richard Nixon.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "IMPORTANT_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec</data>
</edge>
<edge source="United States v. Nixon" target="Watergate scandal">
  <data key="d5">["IMPORTANT_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The United States v. Nixon ruling was important to the late stages of the Watergate scandal.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Issued on July 24, 1974, the ruling was important to the late stages of the Watergate scandal, when there was an ongoing impeachment process against Richard Nixon.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "IMPORTANT_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec</data>
</edge>
<edge source="United States v. Nixon" target="executive privilege">
  <data key="d5">["LIMITS"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The United States v. Nixon decision is a precedent that limits the power of any U.S. president to claim executive privilege.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "\"United States v. Nixon\" is considered a crucial precedent limiting the power of any U.S. president to claim executive privilege.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "LIMITS"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Richard Nixon" target="District Court">
  <data key="d5">["ORDERED_TO_DELIVER_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">President Richard Nixon was ordered to deliver presidential tape recordings and other subpoenaed materials to the District Court.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "United States v. Nixon, 418 U.S. 683 (1974), was a landmark United States Supreme Court decision which resulted in a unanimous 8–0 ruling against President Richard Nixon, ordering him to deliver presidential tape recordings and other subpoenaed materials to the District Court.", "chunk_id": "2d33ed84f83873de", "doc_id": "hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec", "type": "ORDERED_TO_DELIVER_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c019fb6db7dcbec</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Richard Nixon" target="United States">
  <data key="d5">["ORDERED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Richard Nixon, as President, was associated with the bombing campaign by the United States.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "On the memo, in his own handwriting, Nixon described the decade-long bombing campaign by the United States in Southeast Asia as a \"failure,\" having achieved \"zilch,\" despite public comments to the contrary.", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50", "type": "ORDERED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Richard Nixon" target="Dan Rather">
  <data key="d5">["INTERVIEWED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Richard Nixon was interviewed by Dan Rather.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Just the day before, January 2, Nixon told CBS News reporter Dan Rather in an interview that the bombing was \"very, very effective.\"", "chunk_id": "51c2cb97c1a1900f", "doc_id": "hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50", "type": "INTERVIEWED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7681eb8af0f9cd50</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Richard Nixon" target="United States vice presidential selection, 1973">
  <data key="d5">["INITIATED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Richard Nixon was responsible for selecting a new vice president in 1973.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Republican President Richard Nixon thus had the task of selecting a vice president who could receive the majority support of both houses of Congress.", "chunk_id": "54142f25de20997e", "doc_id": "hp_adfdee30bf450e7d", "type": "INITIATED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_adfdee30bf450e7d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Richard Nixon" target="President of the United States">
  <data key="d5">["HELD_POSITION"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Richard Nixon held the position of President of the United States.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "In 1974, Ford ascended to the presidency after the Watergate scandal led to the resignation of President Nixon.", "chunk_id": "54142f25de20997e", "doc_id": "hp_adfdee30bf450e7d", "type": "HELD_POSITION"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_adfdee30bf450e7d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Watergate scandal" target="Richard Nixon">
  <data key="d5">["LED_TO_RESIGNATION_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Watergate scandal led to the resignation of President Nixon.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "In 1974, Ford ascended to the presidency after the Watergate scandal led to the resignation of President Nixon.", "chunk_id": "54142f25de20997e", "doc_id": "hp_adfdee30bf450e7d", "type": "LED_TO_RESIGNATION_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_adfdee30bf450e7d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liriodendron tulipifera" target="Liriodendron">
  <data key="d5">["PART_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Liriodendron tulipifera is the Western Hemisphere representative of the two-species genus Liriodendron.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Liriodendron tulipifera—known as the tulip tree, American tulip tree, tuliptree, tulip poplar, whitewood, fiddletree, and yellow-poplar—is the Western Hemisphere representative of the two-species genus \"Liriodendron,\" and the tallest eastern hardwood.", "chunk_id": "976ede3d58522907", "doc_id": "hp_2c1beb2f73cca8d4", "type": "PART_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c1beb2f73cca8d4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Liriodendron tulipifera" target="eastern hardwood">
  <data key="d5">["IS_A"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Liriodendron tulipifera is described as the tallest eastern hardwood.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Liriodendron tulipifera—known as the tulip tree, American tulip tree, tuliptree, tulip poplar, whitewood, fiddletree, and yellow-poplar—is the Western Hemisphere representative of the two-species genus \"Liriodendron,\" and the tallest eastern hardwood.", "chunk_id": "976ede3d58522907", "doc_id": "hp_2c1beb2f73cca8d4", "type": "IS_A"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_2c1beb2f73cca8d4</data>
</edge>
