<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="domesticated Bactrian camel">
  <data key="d5">["RELATED_TO", "SUB_SPECIES_OF", "SEPARATE_SPECIES_FROM"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The wild Bactrian camel is closely related to the domesticated Bactrian camel.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is closely related to the domesticated Bactrian camel (\"Camelus bactrianus\").", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "RELATED_TO"}, {"text": "While previously considered to be a subspecies (\"Camelus bactrianus ferus\") of the Bactrian camel, the latest research establishes the wild Bactrian camel as a separate species from the domesticated Bactrian camel due to its distinct genetic makeup.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "SUB_SPECIES_OF"}, {"text": "While previously considered to be a subspecies (\"Camelus bactrianus ferus\") of the Bactrian camel, the latest research establishes the wild Bactrian camel as a separate species from the domesticated Bactrian camel due to its distinct genetic makeup.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "SEPARATE_SPECIES_FROM"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="even-toed ungulates">
  <data key="d5">["IS_A"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels are large, even-toed ungulates.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "They are both large, even-toed ungulates native to the steppes of central Asia, with a double hump (small and pyramid-shaped).", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "IS_A"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="steppes of central Asia">
  <data key="d5">["NATIVE_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels are native to the steppes of central Asia.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "They are both large, even-toed ungulates native to the steppes of central Asia, with a double hump (small and pyramid-shaped).", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "NATIVE_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="domestication">
  <data key="d5">["DESCENDED_FROM", "DIVERGED_BEFORE"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels were previously considered to have descended from domesticated Bactrian camels.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Until recently, wild Bactrian camels were considered to have descended from domesticated Bactrian camels that became feral after escaping from captivity or being returned to the wild.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "DESCENDED_FROM"}, {"text": "However, a 1.9% difference in mitochondrial DNA suggests a divergence date of 0.7 to 1.5 million years ago, long before the start of domestication.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "DIVERGED_BEFORE"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="mitochondrial DNA">
  <data key="d5">["DIFFERENCE_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">A 1.9% difference in mitochondrial DNA suggests a divergence date for wild Bactrian camels.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "However, a 1.9% difference in mitochondrial DNA suggests a divergence date of 0.7 to 1.5 million years ago, long before the start of domestication.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "DIFFERENCE_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="Gobi Desert">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels are restricted to remote regions of the Gobi Desert.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is restricted in the wild to remote regions of the Gobi and Taklamakan Deserts of Mongolia and Xinjiang.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="Taklamakan Desert">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels are restricted to remote regions of the Taklamakan Desert.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is restricted in the wild to remote regions of the Gobi and Taklamakan Deserts of Mongolia and Xinjiang.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="Mongolia">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN", "LOCATED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Wild Bactrian camels are found in remote regions of Mongolia.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is restricted in the wild to remote regions of the Gobi and Taklamakan Deserts of Mongolia and Xinjiang.", "chunk_id": "c4aa14b79da7a5bc", "doc_id": "hp_353e9e34cd9559b7", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}, {"text": "The three surviving species of camel are the dromedary, or one-humped camel (\"C. dromedarius\"), which inhabits the Middle East and the Horn of Africa; the Bactrian, or two-humped camel (\"C. bactrianus\"), which inhabits Central Asia; and the critically endangered wild Bactrian camel (\"C. ferus\") that has limited populations in remote areas of northwest China and Mongolia.", "chunk_id": "0f4c26b19727d970", "doc_id": "hp_706e5d69c6cb6d2b", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_353e9e34cd9559b7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="wild Bactrian camel" target="Xinjiang">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN"]</data>
