<edge source="Archbold Gymnasium" target="John Dustin Archbold">
  <data key="d5">["FUNDED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Archbold Gymnasium was built with money donated by John Dustin Archbold.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It was built in 1908 with money donated by John Dustin Archbold, a major benefactor of the university, who also funded the building of Archbold Stadium, just to the west of the gymnasium (now the site of the Carrier Dome).", "chunk_id": "9a89f86e35e64ab2", "doc_id": "hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8", "type": "FUNDED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Archbold Gymnasium" target="Manley Field House">
  <data key="d5">["SUCCESSOR_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Manley Field House became the home to the men's basketball team, replacing Archbold Gymnasium.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It served as the home to the men's basketball team until the building of the Manley Field House in 1962, with the exception of the time from January, 1947 to 1949.", "chunk_id": "9a89f86e35e64ab2", "doc_id": "hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8", "type": "SUCCESSOR_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Archbold Gymnasium" target="Flanagan Gymnasium">
  <data key="d5">["NEIGHBOR_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Flanagan Gymnasium is a neighboring gymnasium to Archbold Gymnasium.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The building was rebuilt from 1948 to 1949, and a southern addition was made in 1952, as well as a neighboring gymnasium (Flanagan Gymnasium), which was built in 1989.", "chunk_id": "9a89f86e35e64ab2", "doc_id": "hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8", "type": "NEIGHBOR_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_b8ec94a3c00016f8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gnetophyta" target="gymnosperms">
  <data key="d5">["PART_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Gnetophyta is a division of plants grouped within the gymnosperms.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Gnetophyta is a division of plants, grouped within the gymnosperms (which also includes conifers, cycads, and ginkgos), that consists of some 70 species across the three relict genera: \"Gnetum\" (family Gnetaceae), \"Welwitschia\" (family Welwitschiaceae), and \"Ephedra\" (family Ephedraceae).", "chunk_id": "e8e7eb907eb94958", "doc_id": "hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33", "type": "PART_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gnetophyta" target="Tertiary">
  <data key="d5">["DOMINANT_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Gnetophyta were diverse and dominant in the Tertiary period.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Though diverse and dominant in the Tertiary, only three families, each containing a single genus, are still alive today.", "chunk_id": "e8e7eb907eb94958", "doc_id": "hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33", "type": "DOMINANT_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gnetophyta" target="flowering plants">
  <data key="d5">["SIMILAR_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Gnetophytes were once thought to be the closest gymnosperm relatives to flowering plants due to similar vessel elements.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Because of this, gnetophytes were once thought to be the closest gymnosperm relatives to flowering plants, but more recent molecular studies have largely disproven this hypothesis.", "chunk_id": "e8e7eb907eb94958", "doc_id": "hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33", "type": "SIMILAR_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33</data>
</edge>
<edge source="gymnosperms" target="conifers">
  <data key="d5">["INCLUDES"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Gymnosperms include conifers.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Gnetophyta is a division of plants, grouped within the gymnosperms (which also includes conifers, cycads, and ginkgos), that consists of some 70 species across the three relict genera: \"Gnetum\" (family Gnetaceae), \"Welwitschia\" (family Welwitschiaceae), and \"Ephedra\" (family Ephedraceae).", "chunk_id": "e8e7eb907eb94958", "doc_id": "hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33", "type": "INCLUDES"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3ea3f73701bd3b33</data>
</edge>
