<edge source="Galiens li Restorés" target="chivalric romance">
  <data key="d5">["BORROWS_FROM"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Galiens li Restorés borrows heavily from the genre of chivalric romance.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Galiens li Restorés, or Galien le Restoré or Galien rhétoré (in English, \"Galien the Restored\"), is an Old French \"chanson de geste\" which borrows heavily from chivalric romance.", "chunk_id": "850408ca66a7d9e6", "doc_id": "hp_3fac05a2ac9ec6d2", "type": "BORROWS_FROM"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3fac05a2ac9ec6d2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Galiens li Restorés" target="Pèlerinage de Charlemagne">
  <data key="d5">["LINKED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Galiens li Restorés is closely linked to the earlier "chanson de geste" "Pèlerinage de Charlemagne".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The story—which is closely linked to the earlier \"chansons de geste\" \"Pèlerinage de Charlemagne\" and \"The Song of Roland\" (especially in the latter's rhymed version)—tells of the adventures of Galien, son of the hero Olivier and of Jacqueline, the daughter of the (fictional) emperor Hugon (Hue the Strong) of Constantinople.", "chunk_id": "850408ca66a7d9e6", "doc_id": "hp_3fac05a2ac9ec6d2", "type": "LINKED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_3fac05a2ac9ec6d2</data>
</edge>
