  <data key="d5">["BASED_ON"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Massenet's Manon is based on Abbé Prévost's novel Manon Lescaut.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Manon Lescaut is an opera or opéra comique in 3 acts by Daniel Auber to a libretto by Eugène Scribe, and, like Puccini's \"Manon Lescaut\" and Massenet's \"Manon\", is based on the Abbé Prévost's novel \"Manon Lescaut\".", "chunk_id": "62ee8e762a5f8458", "doc_id": "hp_51938f55ccd7098e", "type": "BASED_ON"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51938f55ccd7098e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Music for Electric Metronomes" target="electric metronome">
  <data key="d5">["INVOLVES"]</data>
  <data key="d6">'Music for Electric Metronomes' involves the manipulation of electric metronomes.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The piece involves the manipulation of electric metronomes, followed by various unspecified sounds and actions.", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "INVOLVES"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Music for Electric Metronomes" target="graphic notation">
  <data key="d5">["USES"]</data>
  <data key="d6">'Music for Electric Metronomes' uses graphic notation.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Because the graphic notation of score (which is a series of dashes, lines, and numbers in an erratic pattern of connected paths) leaves a lot of room for personal interpretation and expression, each performance is unique, and almost certainly cannot be reproduced.", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "USES"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Music for Electric Metronomes" target="Toshi Ichiyanagi: 1960's &amp; 1990's">
  <data key="d5">["RECORDED_ON"]</data>
  <data key="d6">'Music for Electric Metronomes' has been recorded on the album 'Toshi Ichiyanagi: 1960's &amp; 1990's'.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It has been recorded on the album \"Toshi Ichiyanagi: 1960's &amp; 1990's\".", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "RECORDED_ON"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Music for Electric Metronomes" target="Poème symphonique">
  <data key="d5">["COMPARED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">'Music for Electric Metronomes' was described as a prelude to György Ligeti's 'Poème symphonique'.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "In a review of a performance by the S.E.M. Ensemble in 1992 at the Paula Cooper Gallery, the music critic of \"The New York Times\", Alex Ross, described the piece as \"merely a timid, spastic prelude to György Ligeti's monumental \"Poème symphonique\" for 100 metronomes\".", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "COMPARED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Toshi Ichiyanagi" target="Music for Electric Metronomes">
  <data key="d5">["COMPOSED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Toshi Ichiyanagi composed 'Music for Electric Metronomes'.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Music for Electric Metronomes is an avant-garde aleatoric composition written in 1960 by Japanese composer Toshi Ichiyanagi for any number of performers between three and eight.", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "COMPOSED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Toshi Ichiyanagi" target="Fluxus">
  <data key="d5">["AFFILIATED_WITH"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Toshi Ichiyanagi was affiliated with Fluxus.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The piece involves the manipulation of electric metronomes, followed by various unspecified sounds and actions.", "chunk_id": "90b05205269973c5", "doc_id": "hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5", "type": "AFFILIATED_WITH"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_51b683ecaa90fcc5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Toshi Ichiyanagi" target="Tomojiro Ikenouchi">
  <data key="d5">["STUDIED_WITH"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Toshi Ichiyanagi studied with Tomojiro Ikenouchi.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "He studied with Tomojiro Ikenouchi, Kishio Hirao, and John Cage.", "chunk_id": "0d8ff17508c0bbc6", "doc_id": "hp_63ccc0ae800eae3c", "type": "STUDIED_WITH"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_63ccc0ae800eae3c</data>
</edge>
