<edge source="Zeltnera" target="Centaurium">
  <data key="d5">["SPLIT_FROM", "SUCCESSOR_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The genus Zeltnera was created from a split of the genus Centaurium.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It was erected in 2004 when the genus \"Centaurium\" (the centauries) was split.", "chunk_id": "8be93ea5f19db826", "doc_id": "hp_662cb87fc39342e0", "type": "SPLIT_FROM"}, {"text": "The new name \"Zeltnera\" was given to this genus, which contains most of the North American centauries.", "chunk_id": "8be93ea5f19db826", "doc_id": "hp_662cb87fc39342e0", "type": "SUCCESSOR_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_662cb87fc39342e0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Centaurium" target="centauries">
  <data key="d5">["SYNONYM_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Centaurium is the scientific name for the plants commonly known as centauries.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It was erected in 2004 when the genus \"Centaurium\" (the centauries) was split.", "chunk_id": "8be93ea5f19db826", "doc_id": "hp_662cb87fc39342e0", "type": "SYNONYM_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_662cb87fc39342e0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gyrandra" target="Centaurium">
  <data key="d5">["SPLIT_FROM"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The genus Gyrandra was formed from a part of the original Centaurium genus.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The Mexican species now belong to genus \"Gyrandra\", and the Mediterranean and Australian plants are in genus \"Schenkia\".", "chunk_id": "8be93ea5f19db826", "doc_id": "hp_662cb87fc39342e0", "type": "SPLIT_FROM"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_662cb87fc39342e0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Schenkia" target="Centaurium">
  <data key="d5">["SPLIT_FROM"]</data>
