<edge source="Wild Mountain Thyme" target="Purple Heather">
  <data key="d5">["SYNONYM_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">"Purple Heather" is an alternative name for "Wild Mountain Thyme".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "\"Wild Mountain Thyme\" (also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\") is a Scottish folk song that was collected by Francis McPeake 1st, who wrote the song himself for his wife.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514", "type": "SYNONYM_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7778e88209c46514</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wild Mountain Thyme" target="A Pair of Brown Eyes">
  <data key="d5">["MELODY_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The single "A Pair of Brown Eyes" was composed on the melody of "Wild Mountain Thyme".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It featured on the band's second album, \"Rum Sodomy &amp; the Lash\", and was composed by Pogues front man Shane MacGowan, on the melody of \"Wild Mountain Thyme\", also known as \"Will Ye Go Lassie Go,\" a song by Francis McPeake in a traditional Irish folk style.", "chunk_id": "b52105e0cc7ef21b", "doc_id": "hp_da38acdde66d2037", "type": "MELODY_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_da38acdde66d2037</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Wild Mountain Thyme" target="Will Ye Go Lassie Go">
  <data key="d5">["SYNONYM_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?" is an alternative name for "Wild Mountain Thyme".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "\"Wild Mountain Thyme\" (also known as \"Purple Heather\" and \"Will Ye Go, Lassie, Go?\") is a Scottish folk song that was collected by Francis McPeake 1st, who wrote the song himself for his wife.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514", "type": "SYNONYM_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7778e88209c46514</data>
</edge>
