<edge source="The Braes of Balquhither" target="Wild Mountain Thyme">
  <data key="d5">["VARIANT_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The lyrics and melody of "Wild Mountain Thyme" are a variant of "The Braes of Balquhither".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The lyrics and melody are a variant of the song \"The Braes of Balquhither\" by Scottish poet Robert Tannahill (1774–1810), a contemporary of Robert Burns.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514", "type": "VARIANT_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7778e88209c46514</data>
</edge>
<edge source="The Braes of Balquhither" target="Scottish Minstrel">
  <data key="d5">["PUBLISHED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Tannahill's original song "The Braes of Balquhither" was first published in "Scottish Minstrel".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Tannahill's original song, first published in Robert Archibald Smith's \"Scottish Minstrel\" (1821–24), is about the hills (\"braes\") around Balquhidder near Lochearnhead.", "chunk_id": "1fcb72fd1832f8a4", "doc_id": "hp_7778e88209c46514", "type": "PUBLISHED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_7778e88209c46514</data>
</edge>
