  <data key="d5">["STARRED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Nargis starred in the film Shree 420.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Shree 420 (\"Hindi\" श्री ४२०, translation: \"Sir 420\", also transliterated as Shri 420) is a 1955 Hindi film, directed and produced by Raj Kapoor, written by Khwaja Ahmad Abbas, and starring Raj Kapoor and Nargis.", "chunk_id": "0a8e3d3f676d8b89", "doc_id": "hp_8c200a803fef76a7", "type": "STARRED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c200a803fef76a7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nargis" target="Aag (1948 film)">
  <data key="d5">["STARS_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Nargis starred in the 1948 film Aag.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Nargis, Premnath, Nigar Sultana and Kamini Kaushal also starred in supporting roles.", "chunk_id": "4df4b5295e67f801", "doc_id": "hp_b8f974e5ce544d7e", "type": "STARS_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_b8f974e5ce544d7e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nargis" target="Raj Kapoor">
  <data key="d5">["APPEARED_TOGETHER_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Nargis and Raj Kapoor appeared together for the first time in the film Aag.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "This was the first film in which Raj Kapoor and Nargis appeared together.", "chunk_id": "4df4b5295e67f801", "doc_id": "hp_b8f974e5ce544d7e", "type": "APPEARED_TOGETHER_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_b8f974e5ce544d7e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Section 420 of the Indian Penal Code" target="cheating">
  <data key="d5">["PRESCRIBES_PUNISHMENT_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Section 420 of the Indian Penal Code prescribes punishment for the offense of cheating.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The number 420 refers to Section 420 of the Indian Penal Code, which prescribes the punishment for the offence of cheating; hence, \"Mr. 420\" is a derogatory term for a cheat.", "chunk_id": "0a8e3d3f676d8b89", "doc_id": "hp_8c200a803fef76a7", "type": "PRESCRIBES_PUNISHMENT_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c200a803fef76a7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Charlie Chaplin" target="Raj Kapoor">
  <data key="d5">["INFLUENCED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Charlie Chaplin's "little tramp" character influenced Raj Kapoor's character in Shree 420.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Kapoor's character is influenced by Charlie Chaplin's \"little tramp\", much like Kapoor's character in his 1951 \"Awaara\".", "chunk_id": "0a8e3d3f676d8b89", "doc_id": "hp_8c200a803fef76a7", "type": "INFLUENCED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c200a803fef76a7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shankar Jaikishan" target="Shree 420">
  <data key="d5">["COMPOSED_MUSIC_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Shankar Jaikishan composed the music for the film Shree 420.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The music was composed by the team of Shankar Jaikishan, and the lyrics were penned by Shailendra.", "chunk_id": "0a8e3d3f676d8b89", "doc_id": "hp_8c200a803fef76a7", "type": "COMPOSED_MUSIC_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c200a803fef76a7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Shailendra" target="Shree 420">
  <data key="d5">["WROTE_LYRICS_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Shailendra penned the lyrics for the film Shree 420.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The music was composed by the team of Shankar Jaikishan, and the lyrics were penned by Shailendra.", "chunk_id": "0a8e3d3f676d8b89", "doc_id": "hp_8c200a803fef76a7", "type": "WROTE_LYRICS_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c200a803fef76a7</data>
</edge>
<edge source="John Dustin Archbold" target="Leesburg, Ohio">
  <data key="d5">["BORN_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">John Dustin Archbold was born in Leesburg, Ohio.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "John Dustin Archbold (July 26, 1848 in Leesburg, Ohio – December 6, 1916 in Tarrytown, New York) was an American capitalist and one of the United States' earliest oil refiners.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "BORN_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="John Dustin Archbold" target="Tarrytown, New York">
  <data key="d5">["DIED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">John Dustin Archbold died in Tarrytown, New York.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "John Dustin Archbold (July 26, 1848 in Leesburg, Ohio – December 6, 1916 in Tarrytown, New York) was an American capitalist and one of the United States' earliest oil refiners.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "DIED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="John Dustin Archbold" target="Standard Oil Company">
  <data key="d5">["WORKED_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">John Dustin Archbold worked for Standard Oil Company.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "His small oil company was bought out by John D Rockefeller's Standard Oil Company.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "WORKED_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="John Dustin Archbold" target="Standard Oil">
  <data key="d5">["WORKED_FOR", "PRESIDENT_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">John Dustin Archbold rose rapidly at Standard Oil.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Archbold rose rapidly at Standard Oil, handling many of the complex secret negotiations over the years.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "WORKED_FOR"}, {"text": "The company was broken up by the Supreme Court in 1911 into three dozen smaller operations, Archbold became president of the largest one, Standard Oil of New Jersey.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "PRESIDENT_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="John Dustin Archbold" target="John D Rockefeller">
  <data key="d5">["ASSOCIATE_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">By 1882, John Dustin Archbold was John D Rockefeller's closest associate.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "By 1882 he was Rockefeller's closest associate, and typically acted as the company's primary spokesman.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "ASSOCIATE_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Standard Oil Company" target="John D Rockefeller">
  <data key="d5">["FOUNDED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Standard Oil Company was founded by John D Rockefeller.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "His small oil company was bought out by John D Rockefeller's Standard Oil Company.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "FOUNDED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supreme Court" target="Standard Oil">
  <data key="d5">["BROKE_UP"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court broke up the Standard Oil company in 1911.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The company was broken up by the Supreme Court in 1911 into three dozen smaller operations, Archbold became president of the largest one, Standard Oil of New Jersey.", "chunk_id": "bc2d20db8c1bfb47", "doc_id": "hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af", "type": "BROKE_UP"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c2bc815dfcb30af</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supreme Court" target="Federal government">
  <data key="d5">["DID_NOT_INVEST_AUTHORITY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court did not grant the federal government the authority to protect freedom of movement.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "However, the Supreme Court did not invest the federal government with the authority to protect freedom of movement.", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "DID_NOT_INVEST_AUTHORITY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supreme Court" target="Ward v. Maryland">
  <data key="d5">["HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court consistently held the position that authority was given to the states in cases like Ward v. Maryland.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Under the \"privileges and immunities\" clause, this authority was given to the states, a position the Court held consistently through the years in cases such as \"Ward v. Maryland,\" 79 U.S. 418 (1871), the \"Slaughter-House Cases,\" 83 U.S. 36 (1873) and \"United States v. Harris,\" 106 U.S. 629 (1883).", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supreme Court" target="Slaughter-House Cases">
  <data key="d5">["HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court consistently held the position that authority was given to the states in cases like the Slaughter-House Cases.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Under the \"privileges and immunities\" clause, this authority was given to the states, a position the Court held consistently through the years in cases such as \"Ward v. Maryland,\" 79 U.S. 418 (1871), the \"Slaughter-House Cases,\" 83 U.S. 36 (1873) and \"United States v. Harris,\" 106 U.S. 629 (1883).", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Supreme Court" target="United States v. Harris">
  <data key="d5">["HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Supreme Court consistently held the position that authority was given to the states in cases like United States v. Harris.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Under the \"privileges and immunities\" clause, this authority was given to the states, a position the Court held consistently through the years in cases such as \"Ward v. Maryland,\" 79 U.S. 418 (1871), the \"Slaughter-House Cases,\" 83 U.S. 36 (1873) and \"United States v. Harris,\" 106 U.S. 629 (1883).", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "HELD_CONSISTENTLY_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cinema Verity" target="Sam Saturday">
  <data key="d5">["PRODUCED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Cinema Verity produced the television series Sam Saturday.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The six-part series produced by Cinema Verity in association with LWT, broadcast on ITV, follows the work of DI Sam Sterne (Ivan Kaye), a Jewish police detective, as he struggles to balance the demands of the job with his private life.", "chunk_id": "991c522ae470bacd", "doc_id": "hp_8c33eb6416b6e498", "type": "PRODUCED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_8c33eb6416b6e498</data>
</edge>
