<edge source="MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc." target="United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit">
  <data key="d5">["HEARD_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit heard the case MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc., 991 F.2d 511 (9th Cir. 1993), was a case heard by the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit which addressed the issue of whether the loading of software programs into Random-Access Memory (RAM) by a computer repair technician during maintenance constituted an unauthorized software copy and therefore a copyright violation.", "chunk_id": "2f0d266511dd6551", "doc_id": "hp_94b359165f10bad8", "type": "HEARD_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_94b359165f10bad8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc." target="Random-Access Memory (RAM)">
  <data key="d5">["ADDRESSED_ISSUE_RELATED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The case addressed the issue of loading software programs into RAM.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc., 991 F.2d 511 (9th Cir. 1993), was a case heard by the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit which addressed the issue of whether the loading of software programs into Random-Access Memory (RAM) by a computer repair technician during maintenance constituted an unauthorized software copy and therefore a copyright violation.", "chunk_id": "2f0d266511dd6551", "doc_id": "hp_94b359165f10bad8", "type": "ADDRESSED_ISSUE_RELATED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_94b359165f10bad8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc." target="copyright violation">
  <data key="d5">["ADDRESSED_ISSUE_RELATED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The case addressed whether loading software into RAM constituted a copyright violation.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc., 991 F.2d 511 (9th Cir. 1993), was a case heard by the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit which addressed the issue of whether the loading of software programs into Random-Access Memory (RAM) by a computer repair technician during maintenance constituted an unauthorized software copy and therefore a copyright violation.", "chunk_id": "2f0d266511dd6551", "doc_id": "hp_94b359165f10bad8", "type": "ADDRESSED_ISSUE_RELATED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_94b359165f10bad8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="United States Congress" target="MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc.">
  <data key="d5">["OVERRULED_HOLDING_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The United States Congress enacted an amendment to overrule the holding in the MAI Systems Corp. v. Peak Computer, Inc. case.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The court held that it did, although the United States Congress subsequently enacted an amendment to to specifically overrule this holding in the circumstances of computer repair.", "chunk_id": "2f0d266511dd6551", "doc_id": "hp_94b359165f10bad8", "type": "OVERRULED_HOLDING_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_94b359165f10bad8</data>
</edge>
