<edge source="Executive Order 9066" target="Japanese Americans">
  <data key="d5">["ORDERED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Executive Order 9066 ordered Japanese Americans into internment camps.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Korematsu v. United States, 323 U.S. 214 (1944), was a landmark United States Supreme Court case concerning the constitutionality of Executive Order 9066, which ordered Japanese Americans into internment camps during World War II regardless of citizenship.", "chunk_id": "c923a57087201126", "doc_id": "hp_ad41e3555a82bb26", "type": "ORDERED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_ad41e3555a82bb26</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Japanese Americans" target="World War II">
  <data key="d5">["INTERNED_DURING", "INCARCERATED_DURING"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Japanese Americans were interned in camps during World War II.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Korematsu v. United States, 323 U.S. 214 (1944), was a landmark United States Supreme Court case concerning the constitutionality of Executive Order 9066, which ordered Japanese Americans into internment camps during World War II regardless of citizenship.", "chunk_id": "c923a57087201126", "doc_id": "hp_ad41e3555a82bb26", "type": "INTERNED_DURING"}, {"text": "Although the Court did not touch on the constitutionality of the exclusion of people of Japanese ancestry from the West Coast—which they had found not to violate citizen rights in their \"Korematsu v. United States\" decision on the same date—the Endo ruling nonetheless led to the reopening of the West Coast to Japanese Americans after their incarceration in camps across the U.S. interior during World War II.", "chunk_id": "615cdfa10474d8b6", "doc_id": "hp_c36e9406d9e10267", "type": "INCARCERATED_DURING"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_ad41e3555a82bb26</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Japanese Americans" target="West Coast">
  <data key="d5">["REOPENED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The West Coast was reopened to Japanese Americans as a result of the Ex parte Endo ruling.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Although the Court did not touch on the constitutionality of the exclusion of people of Japanese ancestry from the West Coast—which they had found not to violate citizen rights in their \"Korematsu v. United States\" decision on the same date—the Endo ruling nonetheless led to the reopening of the West Coast to Japanese Americans after their incarceration in camps across the U.S. interior during World War II.", "chunk_id": "615cdfa10474d8b6", "doc_id": "hp_c36e9406d9e10267", "type": "REOPENED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_c36e9406d9e10267</data>
</edge>
