<edge source="Burning of the Clavie" target="Burghead">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Burning of the Clavie is an ancient Scottish custom still observed at Burghead.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Burning the clavie is an ancient Scottish custom still observed at Burghead, a fishing village on the Moray Firth.", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Burghead" target="Moray Firth">
  <data key="d5">["LOCATED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Burghead is a fishing village on the Moray Firth.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Burning the clavie is an ancient Scottish custom still observed at Burghead, a fishing village on the Moray Firth.", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "LOCATED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="clavie" target="New Year's Eve">
  <data key="d5">["USED_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The clavie is lit as a bonfire on New Year's Eve.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The \"clavie\" is a collection of casks split in two, lit as a bonfire in the evening of 11 January, i.e. New Year's Eve (in Scotland, Hogmanay) by the Julian Calendar.", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "USED_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="clavie" target="Julian Calendar">
  <data key="d5">["OBSERVED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The clavie is lit on New Year's Eve according to the Julian Calendar.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The \"clavie\" is a collection of casks split in two, lit as a bonfire in the evening of 11 January, i.e. New Year's Eve (in Scotland, Hogmanay) by the Julian Calendar.", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "OBSERVED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="clavie" target="Doorie">
  <data key="d5">["CARRIED_TO"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The burning clavie is carried up to the Doorie, the ruins of an altar.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is then filled with tar, lighted and carried flaming round the village and finally up to a headland upon which stands the ruins of an altar, locally called the \"Doorie\".", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "CARRIED_TO"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="clavis" target="clavie">
  <data key="d5">["ETYMOLOGY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The term 'clavie' may originate from the Latin word 'clavis' meaning nail.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "One of these casks is joined together again by a huge nail (Latin \"clavis\"; hence the term, it may also be from Scottish Gaelic \"cliabh\", a basket used for holding combustibles).", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "ETYMOLOGY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
<edge source="cliabh" target="clavie">
  <data key="d5">["ETYMOLOGY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The term 'clavie' may originate from the Scottish Gaelic word 'cliabh' meaning a basket used for holding combustibles.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "One of these casks is joined together again by a huge nail (Latin \"clavis\"; hence the term, it may also be from Scottish Gaelic \"cliabh\", a basket used for holding combustibles).", "chunk_id": "297c3e9ada099ad7", "doc_id": "hp_d679e270f6739fa5", "type": "ETYMOLOGY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_d679e270f6739fa5</data>
</edge>
