<edge source="William Gibson" target="cyberpunk">
  <data key="d5">["PIONEERED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson is widely credited with pioneering the science fiction subgenre known as cyberpunk.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "William Ford Gibson (born March 17, 1948) is an American-Canadian speculative fiction writer and essayist widely credited with pioneering the science fiction subgenre known as cyberpunk.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "PIONEERED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="technology">
  <data key="d5">["EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works explored the effects of technology on humans.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Beginning his writing career in the late 1970s, his early works were noir, near-future stories that explored the effects of technology, cybernetics, and computer networks on humans—a \"combination of lowlife and high tech\"—and helped to create an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet in the 1990s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="cybernetics">
  <data key="d5">["EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works explored the effects of cybernetics on humans.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Beginning his writing career in the late 1970s, his early works were noir, near-future stories that explored the effects of technology, cybernetics, and computer networks on humans—a \"combination of lowlife and high tech\"—and helped to create an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet in the 1990s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="computer networks">
  <data key="d5">["EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works explored the effects of computer networks on humans.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Beginning his writing career in the late 1970s, his early works were noir, near-future stories that explored the effects of technology, cybernetics, and computer networks on humans—a \"combination of lowlife and high tech\"—and helped to create an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet in the 1990s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "EXPLORED_EFFECTS_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="information age">
  <data key="d5">["CREATED_ICONOGRAPHY_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works helped to create an iconography for the information age.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Beginning his writing career in the late 1970s, his early works were noir, near-future stories that explored the effects of technology, cybernetics, and computer networks on humans—a \"combination of lowlife and high tech\"—and helped to create an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet in the 1990s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "CREATED_ICONOGRAPHY_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="Internet">
  <data key="d5">["CONTRASTED_WITH"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works created an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Beginning his writing career in the late 1970s, his early works were noir, near-future stories that explored the effects of technology, cybernetics, and computer networks on humans—a \"combination of lowlife and high tech\"—and helped to create an iconography for the information age before the ubiquity of the Internet in the 1990s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "CONTRASTED_WITH"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="cyberspace">
  <data key="d5">["COINED_TERM", "POPULARIZED_CONCEPT"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson coined the term "cyberspace".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Gibson notably coined the term \"cyberspace\" in his short story \"Burning Chrome\" (1982) and later popularized the concept in his acclaimed debut novel \"Neuromancer\" (1984).", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "COINED_TERM"}, {"text": "Gibson notably coined the term \"cyberspace\" in his short story \"Burning Chrome\" (1982) and later popularized the concept in his acclaimed debut novel \"Neuromancer\" (1984).", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "POPULARIZED_CONCEPT"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="William Gibson" target="science fiction literature">
  <data key="d5">["RENOVATED"]</data>
  <data key="d6">William Gibson's early works have been credited with renovating science fiction literature.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "These early works have been credited with \"renovating\" science fiction literature after it had fallen largely into insignificance in the 1970s.", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "RENOVATED"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Burning Chrome" target="cyberspace">
  <data key="d5">["INTRODUCED_TERM"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The term "cyberspace" was coined in William Gibson's short story "Burning Chrome".</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Gibson notably coined the term \"cyberspace\" in his short story \"Burning Chrome\" (1982) and later popularized the concept in his acclaimed debut novel \"Neuromancer\" (1984).", "chunk_id": "4bb65110bd5235e2", "doc_id": "hp_daedfde9b7ca2260", "type": "INTRODUCED_TERM"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_daedfde9b7ca2260</data>
</edge>
