<edge source="Freedom of movement" target="United States law">
  <data key="d5">["GOVERNED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Freedom of movement under United States law is governed by the Privileges and Immunities Clause of the United States Constitution.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Freedom of movement under United States law is governed primarily by the Privileges and Immunities Clause of the United States Constitution which states, \"The Citizens of each State shall be entitled to all Privileges and Immunities of Citizens in the several States.\"", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "GOVERNED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Freedom of movement" target="Privileges and Immunities Clause">
  <data key="d5">["GOVERNED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Freedom of movement is governed by the Privileges and Immunities Clause of the United States Constitution.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Freedom of movement under United States law is governed primarily by the Privileges and Immunities Clause of the United States Constitution which states, \"The Citizens of each State shall be entitled to all Privileges and Immunities of Citizens in the several States.\"", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "GOVERNED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Freedom of movement" target="Corfield v. Coryell">
  <data key="d5">["RECOGNIZED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Freedom of movement was judicially recognized as a fundamental constitutional right in the circuit court ruling of Corfield v. Coryell.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "As far back as the circuit court ruling in \"Corfield v. Coryell,\" 6 Fed. Cas. 546 (1823), freedom of movement has been judicially recognized as a fundamental Constitutional right.", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "RECOGNIZED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Freedom of movement" target="Paul v. Virginia">
  <data key="d5">["DEFINED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Freedom of movement was defined in the Supreme Court case of Paul v. Virginia.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "In \"Paul v. Virginia,\" 75 U.S. 168 (1869), the Court defined freedom of movement as \"right of free ingress into other States, and egress from them.\"", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "DEFINED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Privileges and Immunities Clause" target="United States Constitution">
  <data key="d5">["PART_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The Privileges and Immunities Clause is part of the United States Constitution.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Freedom of movement under United States law is governed primarily by the Privileges and Immunities Clause of the United States Constitution which states, \"The Citizens of each State shall be entitled to all Privileges and Immunities of Citizens in the several States.\"", "chunk_id": "bd4c3187e0206f8a", "doc_id": "hp_de77e95616995229", "type": "PART_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_de77e95616995229</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Jack Ryan" target="FBI">
  <data key="d5">["WORKS_AT"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Jack Ryan was an agent for the FBI.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "John C. \"Jack\" Ryan (born 19 June 1938) is a former FBI agent and police officer.", "chunk_id": "d4daa9d905f1b0e6", "doc_id": "hp_df0d4a55e8bc0f2f", "type": "WORKS_AT"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_df0d4a55e8bc0f2f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Jack Ryan" target="LA Times">
  <data key="d5">["MENTIONED_IN"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Jack Ryan's beliefs were reported by the LA Times.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "In a report by the \"LA Times\", he stated his belief that the Bureau could reinstate him to a position which would not conflict with his personal beliefs that U.S. involvement in Central America is \"violent, illegal and immoral.\"", "chunk_id": "d4daa9d905f1b0e6", "doc_id": "hp_df0d4a55e8bc0f2f", "type": "MENTIONED_IN"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_df0d4a55e8bc0f2f</data>
</edge>
<edge source="The System of Doctor Tarr and Professor Fether" target="Lunacy">
  <data key="d5">["INSPIRED_BY"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The short story "The System of Doctor Tarr and Professor Fether" loosely inspired the film Lunacy.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "The film is loosely based on two short stories, \"The System of Doctor Tarr and Professor Fether\" and \"The Premature Burial\", by Edgar Allan Poe.", "chunk_id": "c947b5f039c20a78", "doc_id": "hp_dfa620b8112ac9e5", "type": "INSPIRED_BY"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_dfa620b8112ac9e5</data>
</edge>
