<edge source="Camellia sinensis" target="tea">
  <data key="d5">["USED_FOR"]</data>
  <data key="d6">The leaves and leaf buds of Camellia sinensis are used to produce tea.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Camellia sinensis is a species of evergreen shrub or small tree whose leaves and leaf buds are used to produce tea.", "chunk_id": "a5aa45896dba5811", "doc_id": "hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2", "type": "USED_FOR"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Camellia sinensis" target="Camellia">
  <data key="d5">["PART_OF"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Camellia sinensis belongs to the genus Camellia.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "It is of the genus \"Camellia\" () of flowering plants in the family Theaceae.", "chunk_id": "a5aa45896dba5811", "doc_id": "hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2", "type": "PART_OF"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Camellia sinensis" target="tea plant">
  <data key="d5">["HAS_COMMON_NAME"]</data>
  <data key="d6">Camellia sinensis is commonly called the tea plant.</data>
  <data key="d7">[{"text": "Common names include \"tea plant\", \"tea shrub\", and \"tea tree\" (not to be confused with \"Melaleuca alternifolia\", the source of tea tree oil, or \"Leptospermum scoparium\", the New Zealand teatree).", "chunk_id": "a5aa45896dba5811", "doc_id": "hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2", "type": "HAS_COMMON_NAME"}]</data>
  <data key="d8">hp_e59ae7221bb43fd2</data>
</edge>
